  "plan": {
    "goal": "Perfused liver-on-chip clearance study of propranolol in the flow-limited (high-extraction) regime, targeting E ≈ 0.8 so clearance reflects perfusion rather than intrinsic enzyme capacity.",
    "rationale": "Propranolol is a high-extraction drug (Rowland & Tozer: E > 0.7, flow-limited). In the flow-limited regime, clearance Cl = E·Q is set by perfusion rate, not enzyme capacity — this is the regime where a liver-on-chip can report perfusion-dependent clearance. I set the target extraction ratio at E = 0.8 (outlet 2 µM from a 10 µM inlet), squarely in the high-extraction regime. At Q = 1 µL/min, Cl = E·Q = 0.8 µL/min. Channel geometry 400×100 µm × 20 mm (≈0.08 cm² growth area) seeded with HepG2 at 1e5 cells/cm² gives ~8000 cells. At 1 µL/min the wall shear is 0.025 Pa (below the HepG2 physiological 0.05–0.15 Pa range) — acceptable for a PK study where flow rate is the controlled variable, and O2 supply exceeds demand ~10× (no hypoxia). Re = 0.067 (laminar), residence time 48 s. A cell-free (blank) chip control is essential to subtract PDMS/tubing binding from cell-mediated clearance.",
