    "caveats": [
      "Verify propranolol's high-extraction classification against your specific cell line — HepG2 has lower CYP activity than primary human hepatocytes (PHH); PHH is the gold standard for hepatic clearance and may be needed to actually achieve E ≈ 0.8 at 1 µL/min. If HepG2 gives E < 0.7, the study is capacity-limited, not flow-limited.",
      "Run a cell-free (blank) PDMS chip control to subtract drug loss from PDMS adsorption/tubing binding — propranolol is lipophilic and binds PDMS; uncorrected, this inflates apparent extraction.",
      "Confirm the outlet concentration (2 µM) by LC-MS/MS; a negative or near-zero E indicates the flow rate is too high relative to metabolic capacity.",
      "The 0.025 Pa shear is below the HepG2 physiological range (0.05–0.15 Pa); if shear-sensitive endpoints matter, consider a higher flow with a correspondingly lower target E, but that moves away from the flow-limited regime.",
      "PHH are BSL-2 human primary material — confirm institutional containment if you switch cell types."
    ]
  },
